The two ants' paths are monotonic lattice paths. Ant 1 moves from \(A(0,0)\) to \(D(4,4)\) with 4 right and 4 up steps: \(\binom{8}{4}=70\) paths. Ant 2 moves from \(B(2,0)\) to \(C(4,2)\) with 2 right and 2 up steps: \(\binom{4}{2}=6\) paths. Total unrestricted path pairs: \(70 \times 6 = 420\).

The condition "never meet" means the two paths share no integer points (vertex-disjoint). By the Lindström–Gessel–Viennot lemma, the number of non-intersecting pairs for the pairing \(A \to D\), \(B \to C\) is the determinant

\[
\begin{vmatrix}
P(A,D) & P(A,C) \\
P(B,D) & P(B,C)
\end{vmatrix}
=
\begin{vmatrix}
70 & 15 \\
15 & 6
\end{vmatrix}
= 70 \cdot 6 - 15 \cdot 15 = 420 - 225 = 195.
\]

Thus the number of path pairs in which the two ants never meet is \(195\).

\boxed{195}
